=== Ketchup ===
The ketchup had been manufactured by [[Heinz]] for 40 years, from the 1970s until 2013 when Heinz hired a former CEO of [[Burger King]] as its new CEO. Since then, the ketchup has been produced in-house.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Anaya |first=Cynthia |date=July 17, 2023 |title=The Reason McDonald's Ketchup Tastes So Different |url=https://www.mashed.com/1336111/why-mcdonalds-ketchup-tastes-different/ |access-date=July 17, 2023 |website=Mashed.com}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Linares |first=Veronica |date=August 25, 2013 |title=McDonald's ketchup is no longer Heinz |url=https://www.upi.com/blog/2013/10/29/McDonalds-ketchup-is-no-longer-Heinz/9351383059845/ |access-date=July 17, 2023 |website=UPI.com}}</ref> The ketchup has been well received. In a 2022 comparison with the former Heinz ketchup, Bailey Fink wrote on [[Allrecipes.com]] that McDonald's ketchup was a bit thinner than the Heinz variant and as such did not coat the fry as much. Fink also noted that, while both ketchups tasted "tomatoey", Heinz's had a "big punch" of spice and recommended McDonald's Ketchup to those seeking a sweeter, more "tomato-heavy" flavor.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Fink |first=Bailey |date=May 6, 2022 |title=Why Does McDonald's Ketchup Taste Better Than Others? |url=https://www.allrecipes.com/article/why-is-mcdonalds-ketchup-better/ |access-date=July 17, 2023 |website=[[Allrecipes.com]]}}</ref>

==Other products==

===Salads===
McDonald's introduced salads to its menu in 1987.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://corporate.mcdonalds.com/mcd/our_company/mcdonalds-history.html |title=History of McDonald's |publisher=McDonald's |access-date=August 28, 2017 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170819171059/http://corporate.mcdonalds.com/mcd/our_company/mcdonalds-history.html |archive-date=August 19, 2017}}</ref><ref name="WSJ">{{cite web |last1=Haddon |first1=Heather |title=McDonald's Customers Love Salads—Who Knew? |url=https://www.wsj.com/articles/mcdonalds-salads-customers-miss-11659716718 |website=The Wall Street Journal |access-date=October 30, 2022 |date=August 7, 2022}}</ref> Since that time, they have restructured their salad lines several times. McSalad Shakers, a green salad sold in a plastic cup designed to fit in vehicle cupholders, were introduced in 2000 and discontinued in 2003.<ref name="WSJ" /> McDonald's then launched a line of Premium Salads (as part of the [[McDonald's Premium line]]) and later introduced the Bacon Ranch Salad with Crispy Chicken and the Southwest Salad with Artisan Grilled Chicken.<ref name="WSJ" /> All of its salads are part of McDonald's move towards creating a healthier image.

McDonald's discontinued salads during the [[COVID-19 pandemic]] in order to improve order accuracy and service times. In 2022, franchises were given the option to add the Caesar Salad with Chicken and the Bacon and Southwest Style Salad to their menus; however, many franchises declined to offer the new items.<ref name="WSJ" />

In 2024, McDonald's locations in Australia discontinued all salads.{{citation needed|date=May 2026}}

===Sides===

==== Apple slices ====

Apple slices are a common side that usually appear in Happy Meals.

McDonald's added apple slices to their menu because the McDonald's CEO realized that kids were not getting enough nutrition in their happy meals so they cut the amount of fries in Happy Meals from 2.4 to 1.1 ounces and replaced them with a bag consisting of a quarter cup of fresh, peeled apple slices.

"McDonald's Apple Slices are a wholesome, tasty side made from real apples. Specially selected varieties mean our apple slices are always crisp and juicy, making for a tasty snack with 15 calories per labelled serving. Enjoy it as a Snack or Side to your meal!" Said McDonald's.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Apple Slices: Nutritious Snack & Side {{!}} McDonald's |url=https://www.mcdonalds.com/us/en-us/product/apple-slices.html |access-date=December 21, 2023 |website=www.mcdonalds.com |language=en-US}}</ref>

====Fries====
{{main|McDonald's french fries}}

[[File:McDonald's French Fries (2024).jpg|thumb|McDonald's French Fries]]
McDonald's advertises their [[french fries]] as their World Famous Fries.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.mcdonalds.com/ca/en-ca/product/french-fries-medium.html|title=World Famous Fries|publisher=McDonald's|access-date=April 8, 2020}}</ref>  Fries were first added to the menu in 1949, replacing potato chips.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://corporate.mcdonalds.com/corpmcd/about-us/history.html|title=History|publisher=McDonald's|access-date=April 8, 2020|archive-date=September 25, 2019|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190925164621/http://corporate.mcdonalds.com/corpmcd/about-us/history.html|url-status=dead}}</ref>  McDonald's fries in the US were originally prepared using a frying oil mixture of 93% [[beef tallow]] and 7% vegetable oil<ref name="OriginalFormula">{{Cite web|url=https://www.atlasobscura.com/foods/original-mcdonalds-french-fry|title=The Original McDonald's French Fry|website=Atlas Obscura|language=en|access-date=April 9, 2020}}</ref> known as Formula 47.<ref name="Formula47">{{Cite web|url=https://www.popsugar.com/node/43864417|title=Truth Is, No Millennials Have Tried McDonald's Original French Fries|last=Roberts|first=Anna Monette|date=October 30, 2017|website=POPSUGAR Food|language=en-US|access-date=April 9, 2020}}</ref> The use of this oil blend allowed McDonald's to develop their fries' distinctive flavor and crispiness. In 1990, McDonald's was pressured into switching to frying oil that was entirely vegetable oil due to the National Heart Savers Association's campaign against [[saturated fat]]s.<ref name="OriginalFormula" /><ref name="Formula47" /><ref>{{Cite news |last=Saxon |first=Wolfgang |date=April 17, 2004 |title=Phil Sokolof, 82, a Crusader Against Cholesterol, Is Dead |language=en-US |work=The New York Times |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2004/04/17/us/phil-sokolof-82-a-crusader-against-cholesterol-is-dead.html |access-date=December 4, 2022 |issn=0362-4331}}</ref> The new US oil used a natural beef flavor additive; other countries' fries do not include any animal products. Then in 2007, the restaurant franchise switched again to a vegetable oil with less [[trans fat]]s as concerns over the health risks of trans fats grew.
[[File:McDonald's Italia Fries Cheese&Bacon (cropped).jpg|thumb|French fries with Bacon and Cheddar sauce]]

====Loaded fries====
In Australia, McDonald's has sold multiple varieties of '[[loaded fries]]' which consist of a portion of fries with a topping; these toppings include:
* Gravy
