===Happy Meal===
{{main|Happy Meal}}

McDonald's capitalized on [[Burger Chef|Burger Chef's Funmeal]] concept of a children's meal when it introduced the first [[Happy Meal]] in 1977. The original happy meal consisted of a regular sized burger, keebler cookies, a soda, and a cracker jack surprise toy.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Meyersohn |first=Nathaniel |date=October 29, 2022 |title=The Happy Meal inventor says McDonald's didn't want it at first {{!}} CNN Business |url=https://www.cnn.com/2022/10/29/business/mcdonalds-happy-meal-history-trnd/index.html |access-date=December 21, 2023 |website=[[CNN]] |language=en}}</ref> (sometime later known as the Mighty Kids meal). The newer meal includes an [[entrée]], a side dish, a beverage and a toy. Happy Meals include a tiny order fries (around half of a small order of fries) and fruit, optional sauce for fries, and a choice of nuggets, a single patty burger, or wraps. An additional product, Pasta Zoo, was discontinued in 2008. The Mighty Kids Meal contains more food than the Happy Meal, less than the adult meal, and still contains a toy. The toy is usually a [[product tie-in]] with a movie or popular television show.

In mid-February 2012, McDonald's introduced the Happy Meals/Mighty Kids Meals so that they now come with either a hamburger, cheeseburger, four-piece Chicken McNuggets, McDouble or the 6-piece Chicken McNuggets with a smaller version of the small fry for the Happy Meal or a small fry for a Mighty Kids Meal. All kids meals now come with fries, apple slices, and a toy. The apple slices have been reduced from eight slices to only four and no longer come with caramel dipping sauce.
<!--some items were removed due to international requirements-->

:* In the U.S., the main is a choice of hamburger, cheeseburger, or a four-piece order of Chicken McNuggets (or a double cheeseburger or six-piece order of Chicken McNuggets for the Mighty Kids Meals); the sides are [[french fries|fries]] and sliced apples. Milk, chocolate milk, boxed [[apple juice]], and soda are choices for drinks. Some U.S. franchises also include a small cellophane package of McDonaldland cookies. In 2009, McDonald's offered a Happy Meal Wrap, but it was discontinued later that year due to low sales.
:* In the UK, the main is a choice of hamburger, cheeseburger, four Chicken McNuggets and McFish the sides are a choice of fries, carrot sticks or a fruit bag (sliced apples and grapes (February – May, September – November), melon (June – August), or a pineapple stick (December – January)). Organic milk, [[Tropicana Products|Tropicana]] [[orange juice]], apple and blackcurrant [[Robinsons (drink)|Robinsons]] [[Fruit Shoot]], milkshakes, water and soda are the choices for drinks. On May 15, 2019, cucumber sticks were added to the menu, replacing carrot sticks; they were used from May to July.
:* In New Zealand and Australia, the main is a choice of hamburger, cheeseburger, three or six-piece order of Chicken McNuggets, grilled chicken pieces, or a crispy or seared Chicken Snack Wrap; the sides are a choice of a small french fries, yogurt, grape tomatoes or apple slices. Thickshake, boxed chocolate milk, apple or water pop tops, orange juice, and soda are choices for drinks.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://mcdonalds.com.au/menu/happy-meal|title=Happy Meal® {{!}} McDonald's Australia|website=mcdonalds.com.au|access-date=October 29, 2019}}</ref>
:* In Canada, the main is a choice of hamburger, cheeseburger, four-piece Chicken McNuggets, or chicken snack wraps; the sides are a choice of [[french fries|fries]] or fruit pieces. Milk, chocolate milk, [[apple juice]], orange juice, water and soda are choices for drinks.
:* In Belgium, the main is a choice of hamburger, cheeseburger, four-piece order of Chicken McNuggets, McFish or a Ketchup Wrap.

[[File:McLobster sandwich at a Fergus Ontario location of McDonalds.JPG|thumb|McLobster in 2015]]

===McLobster===
This is a [[lobster roll]] served in a hot dog bun with [[lobster sauce]] and shredded lettuce, introduced in 1993. The product had supply issues and was not very profitable nationally. Recently, it was only available in [[Atlantic Canada]] and [[New England]] region in the summer, when lobster is inexpensive.<ref>{{cite news|last=Velasco|first=Schuyler|title=10 fast foods that have disappeared: McLobster|url=https://www.csmonitor.com/Business/2013/0821/10-fast-foods-that-have-disappeared/McLobster|access-date=March 11, 2014|newspaper=Christian Science Monitor|date=August 21, 2013|url-status=live|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140310042515/http://www.csmonitor.com/Business/2013/0821/10-fast-foods-that-have-disappeared/McLobster|archive-date=March 10, 2014}}</ref> In the 2010s, McDonald's had short-term reintroductions in Canada and Italy.

===McCrab===
In response to the McLobster, McDonald's along the eastern coast of the [[Delmarva Peninsula]] (comprising parts of Delaware, Maryland, and Virginia) developed the McCrab sandwich, a McDonald's version of the Chesapeake [[crabcake]], which was available on a limited basis in the early 2000s.<ref>{{cite news|title=McDonald's trying to claw way into crab cake business |access-date=February 25, 2024|newspaper=Baltimore Sun|url=https://www.baltimoresun.com/2003/06/27/mcdonalds-trying-to-claw-way-into-crab-cake-business/|date=June 27, 2003|url-status=live|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240225161437/https://www.baltimoresun.com/2003/06/27/mcdonalds-trying-to-claw-way-into-crab-cake-business/|archive-date=February 25, 2024}}</ref> McDonald's offered a similar sandwich named the Crab Meat Sandwich in the [[San Francisco Bay]] area for a limited time in 2017.<ref>{{cite web |last=Bonar|first=Samantha|title=McDonald's is testing a new crab meat sandwich|url=https://www.today.com/food/mcdonald-s-testing-new-crab-meat-sandwich-t108048|access-date=February 25, 2024 |publisher=Today |date=February 25, 2024|url-status=live|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220126201003/https://www.today.com/food/mcdonald-s-testing-new-crab-meat-sandwich-t108048|archive-date=January 26, 2022}}</ref> At the time, it was billed as the first crab sandwich sold by a fast food chain in the United States, though the company had previously offered the McCrab.

===Poutine===
Originally only to be found in French-Canadian communities, poutine is now found on the menu at most McDonald's locations across Canada.<ref>{{cite web|last1=Ross|first1=Spencer|title=A connoisseur's guide to downtown poutine – a & e|url=http://media.www.mcgilltribune.com/media/storage/paper234/news/2004/01/06/AE/A.Connoisseurs.Guide.To.Downtown.Poutine-578854.shtml|website=The McGill Tribune|publisher=Wayback Machine|access-date=January 13, 2017|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20090220222316/http://media.www.mcgilltribune.com/media/storage/paper234/news/2004/01/06/AE/A.Connoisseurs.Guide.To.Downtown.Poutine-578854.shtml|archive-date=February 20, 2009|date=January 6, 2004}}</ref>

===Angus Snack Wrap===
Wrap with Angus burger introduced to the U.S. market on August 9, 2010. [[Snack Wrap|Angus Snack Wraps]] are available three ways: Deluxe (lettuce, tomato, onions, pickles, American cheese, mayo, mustard); Bacon & Cheese (onions, pickles, American cheese, ketchup, mustard, a strip of bacon); and Mushroom & Swiss (sauteed mushrooms, Swiss cheese, mayo). The Angus Wrap uses half of a regular Angus burger patty.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.financefoodie.com/2010/06/mcdonalds-steps-up-their-style-new.html |title=Forays of a Finance Foodie: McDonald's Steps Up Their Style: New Product Preview Day! [New York&#93; |publisher=Financefoodie.com |date=June 8, 2010 |access-date=July 13, 2017 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160411114915/http://www.financefoodie.com/2010/06/mcdonalds-steps-up-their-style-new.html |archive-date=April 11, 2016}}</ref>

===Brownie Melt===
Similar to a Cinnamelt, it is a rich chocolate brownie with chocolate and white frosting.

===McRice===
Most McDonald's restaurants in Asian countries  serve the product due to popular demand. It is normal rice. Especially in Philippines, rice bowls are a Filipino staple, featuring steamed rice topped with dishes like adobo or sinigang. They are a common fixture on both home and restaurant menus, valued for their simplicity and balance.

===McSpaghetti===
McSpaghetti was launched in the late 1970s as a part of McDonald's dinner menu which included a number of Italian dishes. The dish is still available internationally such as in the Philippines where it is popular and is generally served with McNuggets.<ref name="csm">{{cite news|url=https://www.csmonitor.com/Business/2013/0821/10-fast-foods-that-have-disappeared/McSpaghetti|title=10 fast foods that have disappeared: 4. McSpaghetti|last=Velasco|first=Schuyler|date=August 21, 2013|newspaper=Christian Science Monitor|access-date=March 11, 2014|url-status=live|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140312045305/http://www.csmonitor.com/Business/2013/0821/10-fast-foods-that-have-disappeared/McSpaghetti|archive-date=March 12, 2014}}</ref>

===The McBean burger===
This is a vegan burger where the patties are made with cannellini beans, kidney beans, onions, carrots and green peppers. Available in Sweden.

===Indian menu===
The menu in India has numerous unique items, all of which contain neither beef nor pork:
* Maharaja Mac – Chicken – like a Big Mac, but with two pressed spiced chicken patties instead of beef, served with a mustard sauce. Veg – A vegetarian version of the Big Mac sold at McDonald's India, containing patties made of potato, corn, peas, and cheese. ''Maharaja'' is a [[Hindi]] word meaning 'emperor'.<ref>{{cite web|title=Maharaja Mac – Veg and Chicken options|url=http://www.mcdonaldsindia.net/maharaja-mac.aspx|publisher=McDonald's India|access-date=March 13, 2017|url-status=live|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170313215224/http://www.mcdonaldsindia.net/maharaja-mac.aspx|archive-date=March 13, 2017}}</ref>
* Paneer Salsa Wrap (paneer is an Indian unsalted white cheese).
* Chicken Mexican Wrap
* McAloo Tikki – breaded potato patty with onion and tomato on a plain bun (''aloo'' is 'potato'). Available at select stores in India, Greece, and [[Walt Disney World]] in Lake Buena Vista, Florida.  Since McDonald's does not serve beef or pork in any of its products in [[India]], it has created a few vegetarian options to suit the large vegetarian populace. Thus, the cheeseburger is recast as a vegetarian option and served as an "Aloo Tikki Burger" containing a patty made of potato and peas with a slice of cheese.<ref>{{cite news|last1=Harding|first1=Luke|title=Give me a Big Mac – but hold the beef|url=https://www.theguardian.com/world/2000/dec/28/globalisation.lukeharding|access-date=March 13, 2017|newspaper=[[The Guardian]] |date=December 28, 2000|url-status=live|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170313221241/https://www.theguardian.com/world/2000/dec/28/globalisation.lukeharding|archive-date=March 13, 2017}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Whiting |first=Michael |date=August 28, 2023 |title=Aloo Tikki Burger (McAloo Tikki) |url=https://www.food2spoon.com/aloo-tikki-mcaloo-burger/1465/ |access-date=August 29, 2023 |website=Food2spoon |language=en-US |archive-date=August 29, 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230829233748/https://www.food2spoon.com/aloo-tikki-mcaloo-burger/1465/ |url-status=dead }}</ref>

=== Georgie Pie ===
Georgie Pie is a meat pie sold in New Zealand.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://mcdonalds.co.nz/menu/georgie-pie-steak-mince-n-cheese-pie|title=Georgie Pie: Steak Mince 'N' Cheese {{!}} McDonald's New Zealand|website=mcdonalds.co.nz|access-date=October 29, 2019}}</ref> The brand was also the name of a [[Georgie Pie|New Zealand-based chain]] that was bought by McDonald's New Zealand in 1996 and was revived as part of the McDonald's menu in 2013 due to popular demand.

===Veggie Crunch Burger===
This vegetarian burger was launched in the Singapore market on September 6, 2016. Its patty is made with a mixture of up to seven types of vegetables, including green peas, carrots, tomatoes, capsicum, beans, etc. However, its dressings contain garlic and eggs. The patty is fried in the same oil used for frying apple pies so it does not come in contact with meat products.

===Signature Collection===
In McDonald's UK, EotF (Experience of the Future) restaurants offer the premium Signature Collection, which consist of The Spicy, The Classic and The BBQ. These burgers are served on a brioche bun, and the patties are thicker and take longer to cook. The collection costs more than the average Big Mac meal, respectively.

===Sweet potato fries===
Sweet potato fries, first released in Chicago.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.foxnews.com/food-drink/mcdonalds-debuts-sweet-potato-fries-at|title = McDonald's debuts sweet potato fries at restaurant headquarters in Chicago|website = [[Fox News]]|date = August 20, 2019}}</ref>
[[File:McPlant - 52209235090.jpg|thumb|McPlant]]

===Non-meat products===
* The '''[[McVeggie]]''' burger – a vegetarian burger on a whole wheat bun. Available in India, Slovenia, Greece, Malaysia, Portugal, and Australia. A special version is available in [[Cheung Chau]] branch in Hong Kong during the [[Cheung Chau Bun Festival]].
* The '''[[McVegan]]''' burger.
* Veggie Clubhouse – first sandwich with a [[quinoa]] patty.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://netzpropaganda.de/veggie-clubhouse-der-neue-vegetarische-burger-von-mcdonalds/ |title=Veggie Clubhouse - der neue vegetarische Burger von McDonald's |access-date=May 17, 2016 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160508210611/http://netzpropaganda.de/veggie-clubhouse-der-neue-vegetarische-burger-von-mcdonalds/ |archive-date=May 8, 2016 }}</ref>
* '''[[McPlant]]''' is a [[meat analogue|plant-based]] line of foods being developed by McDonald's in house.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.cnbc.com/2020/11/09/mcdonalds-to-test-mcplant-which-includes-its-own-meat-free-burger-next-year-beyond-meat-shares-fall.html|title=McDonald's unveils McPlant line, which includes meatless patty co-created by Beyond Meat|first=Amelia|last=Lucas|date=November 9, 2020|website=CNBC}}</ref>  On October 13, 2021, 250 restaurants in the UK started serving the McPlant burger, and it was launched across the United Kingdom on January 5, 2022.<ref>{{cite web| url = https://www.mirror.co.uk/money/mcdonalds-roll-out-mcplant-vegan-25175783| title = McDonald's to roll out McPlant vegan burger in 250 stores today - see full list - Mirror Online| website = [[Daily Mirror]]| date = October 13, 2021}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=xjR_4IMS2Ws |archive-url=https://ghostarchive.org/varchive/youtube/20211219/xjR_4IMS2Ws |archive-date=December 19, 2021 |url-status=live|title=Why McDonald's Doesn't Have A Vegan Meat Burger In The US|publisher=[[CNBC]]|date=June 13, 2019}}{{cbignore}}</ref>
* The '''[[McMuffin]]''' has a vegetarian version named "McMuffin Egg & Cheese" in some countries.

==Breakfast==
{{primary sources section
| date = May 2022
}}
[[File:McD-Egg-McMuffin.jpg|thumb|An [[McMuffin|Egg McMuffin]]|131x131px]]

===McMuffins===
{{main|McMuffin}}

[[McMuffin]]s are McDonald's signature breakfast sandwich. First sold at select restaurants in 1972 as the Egg McMuffin, five years before a breakfast menu officially went into effect, it consists of a fried egg, [[back bacon]] (called Canadian bacon in the United States) and American cheese on a toasted [[English muffin]]. Sausage or [[bacon]] McMuffins are also available. A Chicken McMuffin is offered in South Africa and Canada. Double variations of all of the muffins are available in Australia (in select states only) and the United Kingdom. In the United States, McDonald's offers a variety of McMuffins, with the Sausage McMuffin and Sausage McMuffin with Egg being standout favorites.

===Breakfast sandwiches===
[[File:McDonalds Hash Brown.png|thumb|142x142px|McDonald's hash brown]]
McDonald's offers a line of breakfast sandwiches: [[bagels]] (introduced in 1999), [[Biscuit (bread)|biscuits]], and a special type of [[maple syrup|maple]] flavored [[pancake]] called [[McGriddles]]. All can be ordered with sausage, ham or bacon, with an optional choice of cheese and/or egg. Regional meat offerings include fried chicken, steak, spam and bacon. They are an internationally famous sandwich and as such have been well received.<ref name="BreakfastMenu">{{Cite web|url=https://www.mcdonalds.com/us/en-us/full-menu/breakfast.html|title=McDonald's Breakfast Menu|website=www.mcdonalds.com|language=en|access-date=October 29, 2019}}</ref> The McDonald's Hamdesal is a breakfast sandwich which consists of a slice of ham on [[pandesal]], which can be ordered plain, with eggs or with cheese. This sandwich is currently available in the Philippines.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.mcdonalds.com.ph/ourfood/view/breakfast|title=Ourfood|publisher=McDonald's Philippines|url-status=live|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170606162911/https://mcdonalds.com.ph/ourfood/view/breakfast|archive-date=June 6, 2017|access-date=July 13, 2017}}</ref> Australian restaurants also introduced in 2018 the Big Brekkie Burger which consists of a sesame seed bun, BBQ sauce, two rashers of bacon, an egg, a hash brown, a slice of Aussie Jack cheese and two 10:1 patties.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://mcdonalds.com.au/menu/big-brekkie-burger|title=Big Brekkie Burger {{!}} McDonald's Australia|website=mcdonalds.com.au|access-date=October 29, 2019}}</ref>

====Bagels====
In the UK, bagels were offered with jam or [[Philadelphia cream cheese]] until 2019, when the breakfast menu was reduced due to the COVID-19 pandemic. McDonald's UK announced that bagels would be permanently removed from the menu in January 2022.<ref>{{cite web |title=McDonald's bagels and breakfast wraps axed permanently - here's why |url=https://www.walesonline.co.uk/whats-on/food-drink-news/mcdonalds-bagels-breakfast-wraps-axed-22854133 |website=Wales Online |date=January 24, 2022 |access-date=February 11, 2022}}</ref> Bagels with cheese and bacon or sausage on them, and steak bagels are available in the US.<ref name="McDUKBreakfast">{{Cite web|url=https://www.mcdonalds.com/gb/en-gb/menu/breakfast.html|title=Breakfast – Menu & Hours {{!}} McDonald's UK|website=www.mcdonalds.com|language=en-gb|access-date=October 29, 2019}}</ref><ref name="BreakfastMenu" />

Introduced to New Zealand in 2001, breakfast bagels are available in the form of the NYC Benedict (hollandaise, bacon, egg, cheese), and BLT (dressed with mayonnaise).<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://mcdonalds.co.nz/menu/breakfast|title=Breakfast Menu &#124; McDonald's New Zealand|website=mcdonalds.co.nz}}</ref>

===All Day Breakfast===
Since October 2015, McDonald's offers breakfast all day in Australia,<ref name="AllDayMenu">{{Cite web|url=https://mcdonalds.com.au/menu/all-day-menu|title=All Day Menu {{!}} McDonald's Australia|website=mcdonalds.com.au|access-date=October 29, 2019}}</ref> Canada, and Mexico ([[Tijuana, Baja California]] Norte only). However, the full breakfast menu is not available at all hours. Only certain items, such as McMuffins, pancakes and hash browns will be available beyond the usual 10:30&nbsp;A.M. cutoff weekdays or 11:00&nbsp;A.M. weekends.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.npr.org/sections/thetwo-way/2015/09/02/436898274/mcdonalds-you-deserve-a-hash-brown-all-day|title=McDonald's: You Deserve A Hash Brown All Day|date=September 2, 2015|work=NPR|url-status=live|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160301033859/http://www.npr.org/sections/thetwo-way/2015/09/02/436898274/mcdonalds-you-deserve-a-hash-brown-all-day|archive-date=March 1, 2016|access-date=March 7, 2016}}</ref>

All U.S. restaurants had expanded their all-day menu to add Biscuit sandwiches, Egg McMuffins and McGriddles in late September 2016. Despite success with the concept, McDonald's temporarily suspended all-day breakfast in the U.S. in March 2020 due to the [[COVID-19 pandemic]] except for hot cakes and burritos, and by September 2020 it had been reported that U.S. franchisees voted to permanently remove all-day breakfast in order to improve speed of service and order accuracy.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.deseret.com/entertainment/2020/9/24/21454697/mcdonalds-all-day-breakfast-menu-return-drive-thru-covid-changes-when-come-back-eliminated-removed|title = McDonald's All Day Breakfast may never come back|date = September 24, 2020}}</ref>

===All Day Lunch===
In Australia<ref name="AllDayMenu" /> and New Zealand,<ref name="AllDayMenuNZ">{{Cite web|url=https://mcdonalds.co.nz/menu/all-day-menu|access-date=January 14, 2025 |title=All Day Menu &#124; McDonald's New Zealand }}</ref> certain regular menu products are available 24/7, these being:
* 10 Piece McNugget Meal
* Big Mac
* Quarter Pounder
* Cheeseburger
* Chicken McNuggets
* French Fries
* Salad Burger (New Zealand)<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://mcdonalds.co.nz/menu/salad-burger|title=&#124; McDonald's New Zealand|website=mcdonalds.co.nz}}</ref>

===Additional items===
[[File:Big breakfast 20260410 075001.jpg|thumb|Big Breakfast product in Malaysia]]
Additional breakfast items include [[pancake]]s; a platter known as a Big Breakfast<ref>{{cite news|url=http://www.delish.com/food-news/a46090/mcdonalds-gets-rid-of-one-key-breakfast-item/|title=McDonalds Gets Rid Of One Key Breakfast Menu Item—And People Are Pissed|last1=Saltz|first1=Joanna|date=February 21, 2016|work=Delish|access-date=December 21, 2017|url-status=live|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20171208110308/http://www.delish.com/food-news/a46090/mcdonalds-gets-rid-of-one-key-breakfast-item/|archive-date=December 8, 2017|language=en}}</ref> comes with [[scrambled eggs]], a hash brown, sausage or bacon, and a biscuit or English muffin. Since February 2016, breakfast bowls were added to the breakfast menu, available in some U.S. restaurants. [[Biscuits and gravy]] are available in parts of the southern U.S. Oatmeal was added to the breakfast menu on December 27, 2010, after it test-marketed well in Washington, D.C. and some parts of Maryland. In the test market areas, oatmeal started selling in late January 2010. A few American regional variations exist, in [[Hawaii]] and [[Guam]] McDonald's offers local breakfast items such as [[Spam (food)|Spam]], Portuguese sausage and fresh-cut pineapple. Donut sticks were added in February 2019.

Hash browns are available internationally with 'extra value meals' being available with a muffin, coffee and hash brown.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://mcdonalds.com.au/menu/breakfast|title=Breakfast Menu {{!}} McDonald's AU|website=mcdonalds.com.au|access-date=October 29, 2019}}</ref>

==Beverages==
{{More citations needed section|date=March 2018}}         
Beverages purveyed by McDonald's vary regionally.

===Soft drinks===
McDonald's primary soft drink supplier is [[The Coca-Cola Company]], except in restaurants which fall under an overall contract with [[PepsiCo]] such as the [[Crypto.com Arena]] and the [[University of Maryland, College Park]] Student Union. In the U.S. and Canada, [[Keurig Dr Pepper]] supplies [[Dr Pepper]]. [[Irn-Bru]] is available in some Scottish McDonald's locations.<ref>{{Cite web |title=IRN-BRU® - Cold Drinks |url=https://www.mcdonalds.com/gb/en-gb/product/irn-bru-medium.html |access-date=June 8, 2023 |website=www.mcdonalds.com |language=en-GB}}</ref><ref>{{Cite tweet|url=https://twitter.com/McDonaldsUK/status/1266276996436221953|title=Yup! Irn Bru will be available in Scotland ☺️|user=McDonaldsUK|number=1266276996436221953}}</ref> [[Inca Kola]] is available in every Peruvian McDonald's location.

===Self-service soda machines===
Many McDonald's locations have self-serve soda machines. The machines have soda drinks such as Coca-Cola products, as well Dr. Pepper, Fanta and less commonly Pepsi. In many locations, free refills are still allowed. In September 2023, McDonald's announced it will be getting rid of the self-serve soda machines by 2032.<ref>{{Cite web |date=September 12, 2023 |title=McDonald's to eliminate self-serve soda machines at U.S. locations  |url=https://www.cbsnews.com/news/mcdonalds-eliminating-self-serve-soda-machines-us-locations/ |access-date=September 13, 2023 |website=CBS News |language=en-US}}</ref>

===Coffee and tea===
S&D Coffee, Gavina and Kraft supply McDonald's Premium Roast Coffee for McDonald's U.S. restaurants except for the New England area, for which [[Green Mountain Coffee Roasters]] supplies [[Newman's Own]] branded coffee there. Hot and iced tea (supplied by S&D Coffee in the U.S.), [[hot chocolate]], assorted [[juices]] and other regional beverages are available in various markets.

''McCafe'' is an umbrella term for [[latte]]s, [[espresso]], [[iced coffee]], [[hot chocolate]], [[cafe mocha|mocha]], [[malts]], [[smoothies]], and other drinks that are sold in several markets worldwide.

===Shakes===
{{see also|Milkshake}}
[[Milkshake|Shakes]]{{efn|In some markets including the United States, McDonald's, like many other restaurant chains, refers to its frozen dairy-based beverages as "shakes" rather than "[[milkshake]]s" for legal reasons.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.thedailymeal.com/1388708/order-milkshake-at-mcdonalds/ |work=Daily Meal |date=September 9, 2023 |title=Why You're Technically Not Able To Order A Milkshake At McDonald's }}</ref><ref name=SnopesHorrors/>}} are available in all U.S. McDonald's and many global markets.<ref name="SnopesHorrors">{{cite web|url=http://www.snopes.com/horrors/food/mcdshake.asp|title=McDonald's Shakes : snopes.com|author=Snopes|date=March 7, 2016|work=snopes|access-date=March 7, 2016}}</ref> Permanent flavors are vanilla, strawberry, and chocolate; regional or seasonal flavors include [[eggnog]] (during Christmas), honeycomb, Arctic Orange ([[sherbet (U.S.)|sherbet]]), orange cream, [[Shamrock Shake]] (a green, spearmint limited-time offer shake for [[St. Patrick's Day]]), chocolate mint, and [[Rolo]] (available only in Canada and the UK. This flavored shake was also available in the Republic of Ireland during the summer of 2007 for a limited time only). In June 1975, 13 months before the celebration of the [[United States Bicentennial]], McDonald's introduced a blueberry-flavored shake in order to advertise "Red, White, and Blueberry Shakes" for independence day celebrations, which were available through August of that year. The run was repeated in summer 1976, but not since. In the U.S., starting in February 2010, Triple Thick Shakes were rebranded under the McCafe name, along with the addition of whipped cream, and a cherry on top, and a significant reduction in size. In Canada, McDonald's shakes are still sold under the Triple Thick name.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.mcdonalds.ca/ca/en/menu/full_menu/desserts_shakes/triple_thick_milkshake.html#/ |title=Triple Thick Milkshake® |website=McDonalds.ca |access-date=July 13, 2017 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170709085943/http://www.mcdonalds.ca/ca/en/menu/full_menu/desserts_shakes/triple_thick_milkshake.html#/ |archive-date=July 9, 2017}}</ref>

===Alcoholic beverages===
Beer of different brands (varies locally) is available at McDonald's in Belgium, Czech Republic, France, Greece, Italy, Lithuania, Portugal, Romania, Slovenia, and Spain. [[Wine]], Santa Julia of the [[Malbec]] varietal, is available at McDonald's restaurants in the province of [[Mendoza Province|Mendoza]], [[Argentina]], as part of a special menu called {{Lang|es|Sabores Mendocinos}} ('Mendoza flavours').<ref>{{cite web|title=McDonald's and Santa Julia present their classic proposition for the Harvest|url=http://www.santajulia.com.ar/en/mcdonalds-y-santa-julia-presentan-su-clasica-propuesta-para-la-vendimia/|website=Santa Julia|access-date=December 4, 2017|date=January 14, 2017|url-status=live|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20171205042124/http://www.santajulia.com.ar/en/mcdonalds-y-santa-julia-presentan-su-clasica-propuesta-para-la-vendimia/|archive-date=December 5, 2017}}</ref>

===Frozen beverages===
{{As of|2009}}, McDonald's Australia and New Zealand began offering [[frozen Coke]] as part of their menu. It is available in small, medium or large sizes. Frozen Coke is a fixed menu item, with new flavors introduced multiple times a year, such as frozen [[Coca-Cola Vanilla|Vanilla Coke]], frozen [[Fanta]] Sour Grape, frozen Fanta Bubblegum and frozen [[Sprite (soft drink)|Sprite]]. As of 2018 there are now eight permanent Fanta flavours available (at most restaurants) including raspberry, blueberry, grape, lime, vanilla, mango, pineapple and peach. The Fanta flavours can be mixed together using the frozen drinks machines to enable patrons to choose up to 36 different flavour combinations.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://mcdonalds.com.au/menu/drinks|title=Drinks Menu {{!}} McDonald's Australia|website=mcdonalds.com.au|access-date=October 5, 2019}}</ref> In 2012, McDonald's Australia and New Zealand began offering a McFloat. It contained frozen Coke and/or the promotional frozen beverage, a small amount of soft serve.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://mcdonalds.com.au/menu/frozen-coke-mcspider|title=Large Frozen McSpider Coke|publisher=McDonald's AU|url-status=live|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170709221021/https://mcdonalds.com.au/menu/frozen-coke-mcspider|archive-date=July 9, 2017|access-date=July 13, 2017}}</ref>

McDonald's also offers 1% milk, 1% chocolate milk, and apple juice, most often as replacements for fountain drinks in Happy Meals.{{Relevance inline|date=December 2022|reason=relevant to frozen drinks?}}

==Desserts==
[[File:McD-Apple-Pie.jpg|thumb|McDonald's apple pies]]
* Pies were introduced in 1968, and were exclusively deep-fried until 1992, when they were replaced by baked pies in most of the American market.<ref>{{cite news|last1=Lovelace Jr.|first1=Berkeley|title=McDonald's tests out new baked apple pie in North Carolina, California|url=https://www.cnbc.com/2016/07/22/mcdonalds-tests-out-new-baked-apple-pie-in-north-carolina-california.html|access-date=March 22, 2018|work=CNBC|publisher=NBC News|date=July 22, 2016|url-status=live|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180323031333/https://www.cnbc.com/2016/07/22/mcdonalds-tests-out-new-baked-apple-pie-in-north-carolina-california.html|archive-date=March 23, 2018}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|last1=Lam|first1=Charles|title=Is McDonald's Bringing Back Its Fried Apple Pies?|url=https://www.ocweekly.com/is-mcdonalds-bringing-back-its-fried-apple-pies-6616012/|access-date=March 22, 2018|newspaper=[[OC Weekly]]|date=April 6, 2015|url-status=live|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180323030619/https://www.ocweekly.com/is-mcdonalds-bringing-back-its-fried-apple-pies-6616012/|archive-date=March 23, 2018}}</ref> The sole flavor available initially was [[apple]], later supplemented, in certain periods and/or certain markets, by [[cherry]], [[strawberry]] and creme, [[s'mores]], [[sweet potato]], and [[holiday]] flavors, among others. In Canada in the 1970s, [[blueberry]] was standard, as was a mixed-fruit pie called the Great Fruit Pie. Other regional varieties, both permanent and occasionally offered, include [[corn]] (Japan), [[cheese]] (Mexico, Guatemala, Panama, and Costa Rica), [[banana]] (Brazil and Hawaii), [[taro]] (Hawaii, Guam and Thailand), [[haupia]] (Hawaii), [[guava]] (Hawaii), [[mango]] (Indonesia), [[chocolate]] (Indonesia) and [[apricot]] (Russia). Apple and (for a short period) banana pies were available in Argentina, but discontinued in the 90s.{{Citation needed|date=September 2022}}
* A [[soft serve]] [[ice cream]] product is available in several forms, including [[sundae]]s, [[Ice cream cone|cones]] (vanilla or at some locations also chocolate), and as the primary ingredient in the McFlurry. As with many other formulations of soft serve, [[carboxymethyl cellulose|cellulose gum]] is utilized as an extender and thickener.<ref>{{cite web|title=McDonald's USA – USA Core Menu Items by Ingredients |url=http://nutrition.mcdonalds.com/nutritionexchange/nutrition_ingredients.html#6 |work=McDonald's Corporation |access-date=May 29, 2009 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20090426045305/http://nutrition.mcdonalds.com/nutritionexchange/nutrition_ingredients.html |archive-date=April 26, 2009 }}</ref>
** [[File:McDonald's milkshake paper container.jpg|thumb|A paper cup of a McDonald's milkshake]]The [[McFlurry]] is a soft-serve ice cream dessert that has pieces of candy or cookies mixed into it.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://nutrition.mcdonalds.com/getnutrition/ingredientslist.pdf |title=McDonald's Nutrition Calculator |publisher=McDonald's |access-date=July 13, 2017 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160708040813/http://nutrition.mcdonalds.com/getnutrition/ingredientslist.pdf |archive-date=July 8, 2016}}</ref> It was created in Canada in 1995, and was later integrated into the American menu in 1997. The McFlurry flavors have included crushed Oreo, M&M's Minis, Reese's, or [[Mars, Incorporated|Mars]] candy bars like Snickers, Twix, Galaxy, etc., [[Cadbury's]] candy bars like Twirl, Wispa, Creme Egg, Crunchie etc., [[Nestlé]] candy bars like Yorkie, Smarties, Drifter, Rolo, Butterfinger, etc. McDonald's also does limited time only flavors, e.g. for ''[[Shrek the Third]]'' it released the Ogre-Load flavor. In Australia, they had a special range for their 40 years in Australia celebration. The flavors were double choc fudge, bubblegum squash, strawberry crumble and caramel cookie. Also in Malaysia, a unique flavor called FuseofmyLife is offered to customers. The mixing blade for the dessert is actually a specially designed spoon with a hollow handle that attaches to the mixer spindle (except in Australia<ref>{{Cite web |title=Oreo Cookie McFlurry {{!}} Dessert Menu {{!}} McDonald's AU |url=https://mcdonalds.com.au/menu/oreo-cookie-mcflurry |access-date=2025-05-09 |website=mcdonalds.com.au}}</ref> and New Zealand<ref>{{Cite web |title=OREO Cookies® Chocolate McFlurry® {{!}} McDonald's New Zealand |url=https://mcdonalds.co.nz/menu/oreo-cookies%C2%AE-chocolate-mcflurry%C2%AE |access-date=2025-06-15 |website=mcdonalds.co.nz}}</ref> where it is not mixed). The blade is used once then given to the customer to use as an eating utensil.
* [[McDonaldland]] cookies are traditionally available and are similar to [[animal cracker]]s, except the shapes of the cookies are of Ronald McDonald, Grimace, Birdie the Early Bird, and the Hamburglar, among other McDonaldland characters. They are manufactured by [[Keebler]] and are just rebranded animal crackers (Elfin Crackers or Our Family Animal Crackers). In Australia, the McDonaldland Cookies are manufactured by [[Arnott's Biscuits|Arnotts]] and are sold in McDonald's restaurants.
* Freshly baked cookies are available in some markets. Among the cookies offered are sugar, oatmeal raisin, or chocolate chip.
[[File:Cinnamon melts.JPG|thumb|McDonald's Cinnamon Melts]]
* Cinnamon Melts – a popular cinnamon roll type item with cream cheese icing.
* Brownie melts – similar to the Cinnamon Melt, only chocolate-flavored with fudge and cream cheese icing on top. Available only in selected markets.
* The Fruit and Yogurt Parfait – a mix of frozen strawberries and blueberries and vanilla yogurt, sold with a package of granola topping.
* [[Smoothie]]s are available in some locations in either blueberry pomegranate, strawberry banana, or mango-pineapple flavors. In the test market areas, smoothies were introduced in 2009. In Australia, smoothies are no longer sold.
* Pineapple – In many Hawaii stores pineapple slices are served, in a McDonald's branded covered tray, with every meal. They can be left out of the meal, which reduces the meal price.
* Donuts (stylized as Li'L Donuts) – introduced in Canada in 2020 under the McCafé concept. These donuts are snack-sized in portion, with five core varieties (Apple Fritter, Strawberry Jelly, Sprinkle, Maple Caramel, and Boston Cream) in addition to a limited-time seasonal offering.<ref>{{Cite web |title=www4template |url=https://www4.mcdonalds.ca/mccafe_eng/donuts/?region=west |access-date=October 3, 2023 |website=www4.mcdonalds.ca |language=en}}</ref>

==Discontinued food products==

===Burgers===
[[File:McDonald's Angus Deluxe hamburger.jpg|thumb|Angus Deluxe]]
[[File:McDLT.jpg|thumb|McDLT]]
* '''{{anchor|Angus}}[[Angus burgers]]''' – Four types: the Angus Deluxe (two slices of American cheese, sliced red onions and sliced red tomatoes, iceberg lettuce, pickles, yellow mustard, and mayonnaise); Angus Mushroom and Swiss (sautéed mushrooms, two slices of Swiss cheese and mayonnaise); Angus Bacon and Cheese (bacon, two slices of American cheese, sliced red onions, yellow mustard, ketchup and pickles); and the Angus Chipotle BBQ Bacon (chipotle sauce, red onions, pickles, bacon, and two slices of American cheese). In Australia and New Zealand, there are three Angus burgers sold: the Grand Angus (which is a clone of the Angus Deluxe) and the Mighty Angus (which resembles the Angus Cheese and Bacon, but with no pickles and mayonnaise and onion relish replacing the ketchup and mustard). For a limited time only, the Angus The Third, similar to the Grand Angus, was sold, but with no mustard and made with a tomato chili relish and only one slice of cheese. Also, for a limited time, Angus the Great was sold, a clone of the Angus Mushroom and Swiss. In October 2012, an Angus version of the Cheddar Bacon Onion (CBO) Sandwich was made available,<ref name="huffingtonpost">{{cite web|url=http://www.huffingtonpost.com/burgerbusiness/mcshakeup-chicken-selects_b_2789019.html|title=McShakeup|date=March 1, 2013|work=The Huffington Post|url-status=live|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20130304135105/http://www.huffingtonpost.com/burgerbusiness/mcshakeup-chicken-selects_b_2789019.html|archive-date=March 4, 2013}}</ref> consisting of a horseradish-like sauce, grilled caramelized onions, bacon and cheddar cheese. In May 2013, McDonald's stopped selling the Angus burger in the United States.<ref>{{cite web |first=Candice |last=Choi |url=https://www.usatoday.com/story/money/business/2013/05/09/mcdonalds-angus-burgers/2144785/ |title=McDonald's cuts Angus burgers from menu |work=USA Today |access-date=July 13, 2017 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170922062723/https://www.usatoday.com/story/money/business/2013/05/09/mcdonalds-angus-burgers/2144785/ |archive-date=September 22, 2017}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|url=http://www.huffingtonpost.com/burgerbusiness/rip-mcdonalds-angus-third-pounder_b_3246100.html|title=R.I.P. McDonald's Angus Third Pounder|work=The Huffington Post|date=May 9, 2013|access-date=October 18, 2013|url-status=live|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20131026141339/http://www.huffingtonpost.com/burgerbusiness/rip-mcdonalds-angus-third-pounder_b_3246100.html|archive-date=October 26, 2013}}</ref> Angus burgers continued to be sold in Canada until the [[COVID-19 pandemic]] in 2020 when the chain discontinued it, citing supply chain issues.
* '''[[Arch Deluxe]]''' – An attempt to produce a "luxury" hamburger, promoted by a high-profile advertising campaign (introduced in 1996), today considered one of the most expensive failures in McDonald's history. It featured a [[Mustard (condiment)|honey mustard]]-type sauce.<ref>{{cite web |last=McGrath |first=Jane |url=http://money.howstuffworks.com/5-failed-mcdonalds-menu-items1.htm#page=3 |title=5. The Hula Burger – 5 Failed McDonald's Menu Items |date=October 20, 2008 |publisher=HowStuffWorks |access-date=July 13, 2017 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170512173046/http://money.howstuffworks.com/5-failed-mcdonalds-menu-items1.htm#page=3 |archive-date=May 12, 2017}}</ref>
* '''[[Beef Wennington]]''' – A burger offered solely in the Chicago area in 1998–1999, named after former [[Chicago Bulls]] player [[Bill Wennington]]. It featured a single patty topped with cheese, onions, barbecue sauce and a slice of Canadian bacon.<ref>Elliott Harris. "Beefing up his role – Bulls' Wennington latest burger celebrity". ''Chicago Sun-Times''. March 8, 1998. Sports, 14.</ref>
* '''Boselli Burger''' – [[Jacksonville, Florida]], franchises also featured the Triple-Double Burger in 1998, named after [[Jacksonville Jaguars]] tackle [[Tony Boselli]].<ref>{{cite web |last=Times |first=The |url=http://www.jacksonville.com/tu-online/stories/081698/jag_1c8danie.html |title=Jaguars: Advertising war games have definitely begun 08/16/98 |publisher=Jacksonville.com |date=August 6, 1998 |access-date=July 13, 2017 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170520124018/http://jacksonville.com/tu-online/stories/081698/jag_1c8danie.html |archive-date=May 20, 2017}}</ref>
* '''[[McDLT]]''' – The McDLT (McDonald's Lettuce and Tomato) was sold in a novel form of packaging.<ref>{{cite web|title=US4653685 (A)|url=https://worldwide.espacenet.com/publicationDetails/biblio?CC=US&NR=4653685&KC=&FT=E&locale=en_EP#|website=Espacenet|access-date= October 13, 2017|language=en}}</ref>
* '''[[McLean Deluxe]]''' – A lower-calorie Quarter Pounder-type sandwich (introduced in 1991) containing 90% lean beef and 10% water with carrageenan, a seaweed extract, to hold the water and beef together.<ref>{{cite web |last=McGrath |first=Jane |url=http://money.howstuffworks.com/5-failed-mcdonalds-menu-items1.htm#page=4 |title=5. The Hula Burger – 5 Failed McDonald's Menu Items |date=October 20, 2008 |publisher=HowStuffWorks |access-date=July 13, 2017 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170512173046/http://money.howstuffworks.com/5-failed-mcdonalds-menu-items1.htm#page=4 |archive-date=May 12, 2017}}</ref>
* '''Mickey D''' – A {{frac|3}} lb ({{frac|5|1|3}}&nbsp;oz or 150&nbsp;g) burger introduced in 1993 with cheddar cheese, red onions, diced tomatoes and a zesty tomato sauce on a crusty roll.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The New York Times|url=https://www.nytimes.com/1993/03/25/business/company-news-in-lean-times-mcdonald-s-tests-a-beefier-burger.html|access-date= February 4, 2020|title=COMPANY NEWS; IN LEAN TIMES, McDONALD'S TESTS A BEEFIER BURGER|date=March 25, 1993}}</ref>
* '''Cheddar Melt''' – A quarter pound beef patty smothered with grilled onions (sauteed in butter and teriyaki), then topped with cheddar cheese sauce on a light rye bun, offered in 1988, the 1990s, 2004, and 2007. They are still available in Brazil under the name Cheddar McMelt.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://findarticles.com/p/articles/mi_m3190/is_n5_v24/ai_8335143/ |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20090426150043/http://findarticles.com/p/articles/mi_m3190/is_n5_v24/ai_8335143/ |url-status=usurped |archive-date=April 26, 2009 |title=McD brings back Cheddar Melt |publisher=Wayback Machine |issue=5 |volume=24 |access-date=July 13, 2017}}</ref>
* '''[[McAfrika]]''' – A sandwich that contained beef, cheese, and tomatoes wrapped in [[pita bread]]. McDonald's claimed it was based upon an authentic African recipe, but was controversial due to its initial release in parts of Europe during a major famine in Africa. Released in Denmark and Norway during 2002, with a sandwich similar in concept being sold during 2008.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Bhasin |first=Kim |title=12 McDonald's Menu Items That Failed Spectacularly |url=https://www.businessinsider.com/failed-mcdonalds-items-2011-8 |access-date=March 8, 2023 |website=Business Insider |language=en-US}}</ref>

===Fish===
* '''Catfish Sandwich''' – A sandwich made with a catfish patty, lettuce, and tangy sauce and sold on a McRib-style bun. Sold at McDonald's locations in Kentucky, Tennessee, Alabama, Arkansas, and Mississippi for a brief period in March 1991.<ref>{{cite news|newspaper=The Press-Courier|url=https://news.google.com/newspapers?id=gEBMAAAAIBAJ&pg=1769,4711814&dq=mcdonalds+testing&hl=en|access-date= July 13, 2017|title=McDonald's testing catfish|date=February 14, 1991}}</ref>

===Chicken===
* '''Chicken McBites''' – [[popcorn chicken]] breast with "home-style seasoning" offered through April 20, 2012, in {{convert|3|oz|adj=on|spell=in}}, {{convert|5|oz|adj=on|spell=in}} and {{convert|10|oz|adj=on|spell=in}} versions. Dipping sauces include ranch, sweet n' sour, tangy BBQ, chipotle BBQ, and honey mustard. Spicy Chicken McBites were also offered occasionally in the United States.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.beaconjournal.com/story/business/2012/01/24/mcdonald-s-unveils-chicken-mcbites/10598719007/  |title=McDonald's Unveils Chicken McBites |date=January 24, 2012 |work=[[Akron Beacon Journal]] |agency=Los Angeles Times |access-date=March 6, 2026}}</ref>
[[File:McD-Chicken-Selects.jpg|thumb|Chicken Selects]]
* '''Chicken Selects''' – McDonald's version of [[chicken fingers|chicken strips]]. They were introduced in early 1998 and offered again as a permanent part of the menu in late October 2003. They are sold in three-, five-, or ten-piece sizes and include choices of [[Buffalo wings|spicy buffalo]], [[Ranch dressing|creamy ranch]], [[honey mustard]], and [[chipotle]] barbecue dipping sauces; sauce selections in the United Kingdom are sweet and smokey [[Barbecue sauce|barbecue]], Frank's RedHot Mayo, [[Garlic mayonnaise|garlic mayo]] and sweet [[chili sauce]].<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.mcdonalds.com/app_controller.nutrition.categories.ingredients.index.html |title=McDonald's USA – USA Core Menu Items by Ingredients |access-date=July 13, 2017 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20080513044750/http://www.mcdonalds.com/app_controller.nutrition.categories.ingredients.index.html |archive-date=May 13, 2008 }}</ref> Sold in the United States, Canada, Israel, Ireland and the United Kingdom. Available in Australia under the name Chicken McDippers.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.azcentral.com/business/consumer/articles/0130wsj-wrap30-ON.html |title=McDonald's chicken snack a hit |publisher=Azcentral.com |date=January 30, 2007 |access-date=July 13, 2017}}</ref> McDonald's stopped selling Chicken Selects in the United States in February 2013.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://consumerist.com/2013/02/28/bid-adieu-to-chicken-selects-as-mcdonalds-makes-room-on-the-menu-for-newer-items/ |title=Bid Adieu To Chicken Selects As McDonald's Makes Room On The Menu For Newer Items |publisher=Consumerist |date=February 28, 2013 |access-date=July 13, 2017 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20171014182554/https://consumerist.com/2013/02/28/bid-adieu-to-chicken-selects-as-mcdonalds-makes-room-on-the-menu-for-newer-items/ |archive-date=October 14, 2017}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|last1=Baertlein|first1=Lisa|title=McDonald's dropping Fruit & Walnut Salad, Chicken Selects in U.S.|url=https://www.reuters.com/article/us-mcdonalds-menu-idUSBRE9200VI20130301|work=Reuters|access-date=October 13, 2017|date=March 1, 2013|url-status=live|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20151116054016/http://www.reuters.com/article/2013/03/01/us-mcdonalds-menu-idUSBRE9200VI20130301|archive-date=November 16, 2015}}</ref>
* '''Grilled Chicken Flatbread''' – Grilled chicken, lettuce, tomatoes, and onions with pepper jack cheese and creamy herb sauce in a heated flatbread wrap. It was sold in late 2001, but discontinued in early 2003.
* '''Chicken Nuggets''' – Chicken nuggets were discontinued in Japan in January 2015.<ref>{{Cite web |title=McDonald's Japan Halt Chicken Nugget Sales After Vinyl Scare |url=https://www.rttnews.com/2438092/mcdonald-s-japan-halt-chicken-nugget-sales-after-vinyl-scare.aspx |access-date=April 26, 2025 |website=RTTNews |language=en}}</ref>

===Other sandwiches===
* '''Cuban Sandwich''' – Roast pork and ham sandwich on a long roll with Swiss cheese, mustard, and pickles. One of several Hispanic-inspired menu items briefly sold at South Florida units in 2001.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.orlandosentinel.com/2001/10/28/mcdonalds-tries-adding-hispanic-fare-to-fast-food/ |title=Mcdonald's Tries Adding Hispanic Fare To Fast Food – tribunedigital-orlandosentinel |newspaper=Orlando Sentinel |date=October 28, 2001 |access-date=July 13, 2017 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20171018070821/http://articles.orlandosentinel.com/2001-10-28/news/0110280140_1_cuban-sandwiches-chicken-mcnuggets-mcdonald |archive-date=October 18, 2017}}</ref>
* '''Fried Roast Beef Sandwich''' – In 1968, this product was launched targeting adults. Sold with a package of McDonald's BBQ sauce on the side using the slogan "McDonald's is my kind of place"; it was inspired by a franchisee's version of an [[Arby's]] sandwich. The sandwich failed due to the costs of getting roast beef slicers; no matter how many sandwiches they could sell, it would never turn a profit.<ref name="Love">{{cite book|last1=Love|first1=John F.|title=McDonald's: Behind the Arches|date=1995|publisher=Bantam Books|location=New York|isbn=0553347594|edition=Revised|url-access=registration|url=https://archive.org/details/isbn_9785699197361}}</ref>
* '''Hula Burger''' – A Ray Kroc invention, it featured a slice of [[pineapple]] instead of meat. Originally intended for [[Roman Catholics]] who are [[Fasting and abstinence in the Catholic Church|not allowed to eat meat on Fridays]] during [[Lent]]. It was test-marketed in 1963 alongside the Filet-O-Fish sandwich, with the highest seller being added to the menu full-time (the Filet-O-Fish, a creation of an Ohio franchiser, won).<ref>{{cite web |last=McGrath |first=Jane |url=http://money.howstuffworks.com/5-failed-mcdonalds-menu-items1.htm |title=5. The Hula Burger – 5 Failed McDonald's Menu Items |date=October 20, 2008 |publisher=HowStuffWorks |access-date=July 13, 2017 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170512173046/http://money.howstuffworks.com/5-failed-mcdonalds-menu-items1.htm |archive-date=May 12, 2017}}</ref>
* '''[[Kiwiburger]]''' – Sold intermittently since the 1990s, the Kiwiburger was designed to reflect their eponymous [[Kiwi (bird)|national bird]], [[Kiwi (nickname)|population]], and [[Kiwiana|culture]]. It contains a quarter-pound beef patty, a round egg, cheese, lettuce, tomato, [[beetroot]], onions, ketchup, and mustard. It comes in distinctive packaging with 46 classic Kiwi quotes, referencing [[Kiwiana]], sporting icons, cuisine, and hobbies; such quotes have also been used in promotional material.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.filmarchive.org.nz/sellebration/view.php?id=225 |title=McDonald's Kiwi Burger television advertisement – New Zealand Film Archive |access-date=October 15, 2010 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110714100034/http://www.filmarchive.org.nz/sellebration/view.php?id=225 |archive-date=July 14, 2011 }}</ref>
* '''Toasted Deli Sandwich''' - Known as TDL's, these were sold in Canada between 2005 and 2006. They included Beef & Provolone as well as a Turkey BLT made on small sub-style bread. They required a separate sandwich making station with toasters.<ref>{{Cite news |date=November 1, 2004 |title=McDonald's targets sub industry |url=https://www.theglobeandmail.com/report-on-business/mcdonalds-targets-sub-industry/article18276354/ |access-date=May 16, 2024 |work=The Globe and Mail |language=en-CA}}</ref>

===Pizza and pasta===
* '''Dinner Menu''' – After testing pasta in the South in 1989, McDonald's began testing a pasta-based menu at 40 units across [[Rochester, New York]], in September 1991, including [[Italian-American cuisine|Italian-American]] classics such as [[lasagna]], [[fettuccine alfredo]], and [[Spaghetti and meatballs|spaghetti with meatballs]]. In the early 1990s, a new Dinner Menu was tested for 6–12 months at two locations in New York and Tennessee. It consisted of the pizza mentioned below but also included lasagna, spaghetti, fettuccine alfredo, and roasted chicken as other mains. The side dishes included mashed potatoes with gravy and a vegetable medley.{{Citation needed|date=March 2009}} For the dessert, it included a brownie à la mode or peanut butter pie.<ref name="csm" /><ref name="howstuffworks2">{{cite web |last=McGrath |first=Jane |url=http://money.howstuffworks.com/5-failed-mcdonalds-menu-items1.htm#page=2 |title=5. The Hula Burger – 5 Failed McDonald's Menu Items |date=October 20, 2008 |publisher=HowStuffWorks |access-date=July 13, 2017 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170512173046/http://money.howstuffworks.com/5-failed-mcdonalds-menu-items1.htm#page=2 |archive-date=May 12, 2017}}</ref>
* '''McSpaghetti''' - Starting in 1970, exclusively in the Philippines McDonald's sold spaghetti. The item was discontinued from the menu in 1980, but began gaining popularity in 2021 because a Twitter user claimed that they ordered it from McDonald's one time, and the item was re-introduced in the Philippines and one location in Orlando, Florida. They served it as a classic spaghetti with meatballs and cheese, freshly made with sweet/sour tomato sauce.{{citation needed|date=December 2023}}<ref>{{Cite web |title=McSpaghetti assembly instructions |url=https://accessmcdo.mcd.com.ph/ibose/images/mcdo/Training%202012/GD%20SUPPORT%20STATIONS/McSpaghetti%20Assembly_TSOC_May%202012.pdf |website=Microsoft Word - McSpaghetti Assembly_TSOC_Dec10 |access-date=December 21, 2023 |archive-date=October 25, 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231025023551/https://accessmcdo.mcd.com.ph/ibose/images/mcdo/Training%202012/GD%20SUPPORT%20STATIONS/McSpaghetti%20Assembly_TSOC_May%202012.pdf |url-status=dead }}</ref> Today, its one of the regular main menu items, often paired with regular deals.
* '''[[Pizza]]/McPizza''' – McDonald's has also attempted pizza at various times,<ref name="csm" /><ref name="howstuffworks2" /> with an apple-pie–like McPizza and more conventional McDonald's Pizza. A line of personal-sized pizzas was first seen in the late 1970s in test-market stores near interstate highways around Milwaukee and Madison. In Canada (c. 1992–99), the pizza originally began as a family-sized pizza that was brought out to the table by an employee and placed on a raised rack in the centre of the table. Later it was scaled down to a personal-sized pizza. However, variations have found their way into some international markets such as India (the pie-like "Pizza McPuff"). McDonald's also test marketed a 14-inch, round, traditional-style pizza in [[Evansville, Indiana]], and nearby [[Owensboro, Kentucky]], in 1989.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://business.highbeam.com/409700/article-1G1-7834069/mcpizza-mcdonald-widens-pizza-test-pizza-operators|title=McPizza? McDonald's widens pizza test: pizza operators gird for assault|publisher=Findarticles.com|work=Nation's Restaurant News|date=July 31, 1989|access-date=November 13, 2009|first=Rajan|last=Chaudhry|url-status=usurped|via=[[HighBeam Research]]|url-access=subscription|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160213015436/https://business.highbeam.com/409700/article-1G1-7834069/mcpizza-mcdonald-widens-pizza-test-pizza-operators|archive-date=February 13, 2016}}</ref> By 1991, the McDonald's test markets for pizza had grown to over 500 McDonald's locations before the pizza test was placed on hold.<ref name="Findarticles.com">{{cite news|url=http://findarticles.com/p/articles/mi_m3190/is_n39_v25/ai_11358816 |title=McDonald's developing separate dinner menu, puts pizza test on hold |work=Nation's Restaurant News |date=October 7, 1991 |access-date=February 28, 2009 |first=Milford |last=Prewitt |url-status=usurped |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20090501072816/http://findarticles.com/p/articles/mi_m3190/is_n39_v25/ai_11358816/ |issue=39 |volume=25 |archive-date=May 1, 2009 }}</ref> Pizza was discontinued in most restaurants by 1999. The reason for eliminating pizza from the menu was that it took 11 minutes to cook a pizza, and McDonald's wanted to keep its reputation for fast service. A decade later, two McDonald's locations,<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=aAAnhp_7v1M |archive-url=https://ghostarchive.org/varchive/youtube/20211219/aAAnhp_7v1M |archive-date=December 19, 2021 |url-status=live|title=McPizza! – Tasting the elusive McDonald's Pizza|publisher=Science Tortilla (YouTube)|date=March 14, 2015}}{{cbignore}} – Video on a taste test of a McPizza from one of the two locations still selling it.</ref> one in [[Pomeroy, Ohio]], and the other in [[Spencer, West Virginia]], still offered the family-style McPizza; the Ohio and West Virginia locations ceased serving pizzas in 2017.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.wlwt.com/article/the-worlds-last-mcpizza-was-served-today-in-ohio/12160791|title=The nation's last McPizza was served Thursday in Ohio|date=September 1, 2017|access-date=November 23, 2019}}</ref> In November 2020, only the largest U.S. McDonald's, in Orlando, Florida, features pizza, pasta, and Belgian waffles.<ref name="rd/orlando-mcdonalds">{{cite news |last1=Jones |first1=Meghan |title=This McDonald's Seriously Has the Best Menu in the World |url=https://www.rd.com/article/orlando-mcdonalds-best-menu/ |access-date=October 20, 2022 |work=[[Reader's Digest]] |date=April 9, 2019}}</ref><ref name=".businessinsider/mcpizza">{{cite web |last1=Taylor |first1=Kate |title=The rise and fall of McDonald's pizza, a doomed fast-food icon |url=https://www.businessinsider.com/mcpizza-the-rise-and-fall-of-mcdonalds-pizza-2020-11 |website=Business Insider |access-date=October 20, 2022 |date=November 3, 2020}}</ref>

===Drinks & desserts===
* '''Diet Coke Float and Coke Float''' – In 1991, McDonald's ran a limited-time promotion selling a float made with Diet Coke or Coke with low-fat soft-serve frozen yogurt in a collectable Coke bell glass. It sold for $1.59 with 40 cents from every sale donated to Ronald McDonald Children's Charities.<ref>{{cite news|title=Floats offered at McDonald's|url=https://news.google.com/newspapers?nid=2002&dat=19910522&id=6bciAAAAIBAJ&pg=1463,4263158|access-date=July 12, 2017|work=Beaver County Times|date=May 22, 1991|page=14}}</ref>
* '''Triple Ripple''' – A mixed cone with strawberry, vanilla, and chocolate.<ref name="Love"/>

